# Answer 5 · Fundamental Differences between Gender Studies and Women's Studies; the Status of Women's Studies in Pakistan (CSS 2018)
@py question 2018-2

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Three demands: the deep differences, an example for each, and a focused picture of the field in Pakistan today.
@ask ① WHAT ARE the fundamental differences between GS and WS?
@ask ② SUBSTANTIATE with examples
@ask ③ HIGHLIGHT the current status of WS in Pakistan
(1) Introduction: WS asks what happens to women; GS asks how the gender system shapes everyone
(2) Women's Studies: born 1969–70 from the women's movement; women as subject, knower, beneficiary
(3) Gender Studies: grew from the 1980s; studies relations, men and sexualities
(4) Six deep differences: subject, key idea, politics, viewpoint, theories, institutions
(5) Examples: honour killing, maternal health and politics seen two ways
(6) What they share: feminist roots, criticism of male-centred knowledge, aim of change
(7) Status in Pakistan: NGOs researched first; five centres 1989; degrees to PhD; CSS 2016
(8) Challenges and way forward: donor funding, few teachers, Western books; need local research and integration
(9) Conclusion: GS completes WS; the field has arrived but is fragile
:::

## Decoding the question
**"What are the fundamental differences"** asks for the **deep** differences, not only a change of name. **"Substantiate with examples"** means every difference needs an illustration. **"Highlight the current status"** asks for a focused picture of the field in Pakistan today — institutions, achievements and problems.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Two related fields
**Women's Studies** and **Gender Studies** are often used as if they were the same. They are related — Gender Studies grew out of Women's Studies — but they differ in subject, key idea and politics.

#### ii) The difference in one line
**Women's Studies asks what happens to women. Gender Studies asks how the whole gender system shapes everyone's life.**

#### iii) Pakistan
In Pakistan both names are used, and the field they describe is **established but still fragile**.

### 2. Women's Studies

#### i) Origin
Women's Studies was born in American universities as "**the academic arm of the women's movement**": a course at **Cornell** (==1969==), the first programme at **San Diego State** (==1970==), and the **National Women's Studies Association** (==1977==).

#### ii) Its three features
Women became the **subject** of study, the **knowers** whose experience counts, and the **beneficiaries** of research. Its guiding question was: *where are the women?*

### 3. Gender Studies

#### i) Origin
From the 1980s many programmes became "Gender Studies". The idea of **gender** had grown — **Ann Oakley** (==1972==), **Gayle Rubin**'s "sex/gender system" (==1975==), and **Joan Scott**'s gender as "a primary way of signifying relationships of power" (==1986==).

#### ii) Wider subject
**Men's studies** (**Raewyn Connell**, ==1987==), **queer theory** (about ==1990==) and critics of a single "woman" widened the field to **women, men, gender-diverse people and the relations between them**.

### 4. The fundamental differences

#### i) Subject
Women's Studies studies **women**; Gender Studies studies **gender relations**.
*Example:* one thesis records the lives of **women home-based workers** in Lahore; another studies how **husbands, contractors and the law** together shape that work.

#### ii) Key idea
Women's Studies centres on **women's oppression**; Gender Studies on gender as **made by society** and as **power**.
*Example:* WS asks why women earn less; GS also asks why "providing" is tied to **manhood**, so men who earn less feel shamed.

#### iii) Politics
Women's Studies is **openly woman-centred**; Gender Studies keeps feminist roots but also studies **masculinities and sexualities**.
*Example:* Gender Studies departments now research **men's violence** and **transgender rights**.

#### iv) Viewpoint and theory
Women's Studies uses research **by and for women** and **liberal, radical and socialist feminism**; Gender Studies uses **many viewpoints** and adds **constructionism, queer theory and intersectionality**.
*Example:* a Women's Studies project on rural women might collect women's own life stories; a Gender Studies project might also interview **husbands and elders**, and use **intersectionality** to compare landless and landowning women.

#### v) Institutions
Pakistan's centres of ==1989== were "**Women's Studies**" centres; most university units today are "**Gender Studies**" departments.

### 5. Examples that show the difference

::: table | One problem, two lenses.
@widths 2.6,5.7,5.7
Problem | Women's Studies lens | Gender Studies lens
Honour killing | The victims; failures of police and courts | Also *ghairat* and manhood; family bargaining over "forgiveness"; the law until 2016
Maternal health | Women's experience of childbirth and neglect | Also who decides — husbands, mothers-in-law, male-set budgets
Politics | Women voters and legislators; their obstacles | Also party gatekeeping by men; masculine political culture
:::

These examples show why the difference is **fundamental**. Women's Studies **documents a harm** clearly; Gender Studies **explains the system** that produces it and finds where it can be broken. Policy needs both: proof of the problem, and a map of its causes.

### 6. What they share

Both share **feminist roots**, a rejection of **male-centred knowledge**, the use of **many disciplines** and an **aim of change**. **Tania Modleski** warned in *Feminism Without Women* (==1991==) that "gender" could hide women again — which is why many departments use the joint name "**Women's and Gender Studies**".

::: fig compare | Two lenses on one project.
@vs +
## Women's Studies
- Women at the centre
- Records women's voices and harms
- Names oppression plainly
## Gender Studies
- Relations between genders
- Explains the system behind the harm
- Includes men and sexualities
:::

The two fields also answer different policy needs. A ministry that wants to know **how many girls are out of school** needs the counting and recording of Women's Studies; a ministry that wants to know **why** — and what would change parents' decisions — needs the relational analysis of Gender Studies.

### 7. The current status in Pakistan

#### i) Early research
Research began with NGOs — **Shirkat Gah** (==1975==), the **Women's Action Forum** (==1981==), the **Aurat Foundation** (==1986==) — and with Mumtaz and Shaheed's *Women of Pakistan* (==1987==).

#### ii) Institutions
In ==1989== the **Ministry of Women's Development** set up centres at **Quaid-i-Azam University** and the Universities of **Karachi, Punjab, Peshawar** and **Balochistan**. Many universities now offer **BS, MPhil and PhD** degrees; journals such as the *Pakistan Journal of Gender Studies* publish local work.

#### iii) Achievements
Research helped the laws on **harassment** (==2010==) and **honour crimes** (==2016==); graduates staff the **National Commission on the Status of Women** (==2000==); and the field became a **CSS optional subject** from ==CE-2016==.

#### iv) Renaming
Most centres have renamed themselves "**Gender Studies**", and their research now covers **masculinities**, **climate**, **digital harassment** and **transgender rights** as well as women's legal status — the shift described in part one, happening in Pakistan.

### 8. Challenges and the way forward

#### i) Challenges
**Donor-dependent funding**, **few trained teachers**, a curriculum of **Western books in English**, **weak data**, **social resistance** to a supposed "Western agenda", and **ghettoisation** — mostly female students with little influence elsewhere.

#### ii) The way forward
**Steady funding** through the Higher Education Commission, **PhD training**, **local research** and books in Urdu, and **integration** of gender into law, medicine, economics and teacher training.

#### iii) An honest verdict
The field has **arrived** — degrees, journals, trained people and real influence on law. But it is **not yet secure**: its money comes and goes with donors, and its ideas have barely reached the other professions. Its strength in the next decade will depend on **local roots** and **wider reach**.

### 9. Conclusion
The fundamental difference between the two fields lies in their subject — women, or the gender system — and in the ideas and politics that follow. The examples show that Gender Studies **does not replace** Women's Studies but **completes** it by adding men, manhood and power. In Pakistan, the field has moved from activist research to university degrees and real influence on law; its next task is to **secure its base and widen its reach**. Both names will stay in use in Pakistan's universities — and both lenses will be needed.

## Why this answer scores
- Five **fundamental** differences, each with its own sub-heading and **example**, as "substantiate" demands.
- A **table** and a **comparison figure** show the two lenses side by side.
- The status part is **focused and current**: early research, institutions, achievements, challenges.
- **Key words in bold** in short paragraphs make marking easy.
- The conclusion answers **all three demands** in one paragraph.
